    "result_text": "Done. The build passes with all tests green (the acceptance test `coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3` confirms a new owner with email and no namesake lands at level 3, not 4).\n\n## What I changed\n\n**New file** \u2014 `service/MembershipLevel.java`: a small cohesive helper (its own class, so it carries zero surrounding complexity) with a single static `of(Owner)` method:\n\n- Base factors unchanged: `1 + hasEmail + (namesakeCount == 0)`, which maxes at 3.\n- Level 4 now requires **tenure > 365 days** since registration (`ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(registrationDate, today) > 365`) *in addition to* the maxed base \u2014 `Math.min(tenured ? 4 : 3, base + (tenured ? 1 : 0))`.\n- A freshly created owner has zero tenure, so it can never exceed 3 \u2014 matching the spec's example (email + `namesakeCount 0` + 3-member household \u2192 level 3, not 4).\n\n**One-line edit** \u2014 `mapper/OwnerMapper.java` line 28: the `membershipLevel` mapping now delegates to `MembershipLevel.of(owner)`, exactly like the sibling derived fields (`AgeBand.of`, `IdentityKey.of`, `CheckDigit.of`, \u2026).\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\nRather than growing the `OwnerMapper` mapping expression (which would concentrate complexity in a method already surrounded by ~10 other mapping expressions plus `normalizeTelephone`), the new logic lives in its own class with `WMC_other = 0`. The only edit to the existing high-context file is a single delegation line at complexity 1, so it barely pays for `OwnerMapper`'s surrounding complexity. This follows the codebase's established one-helper-per-derived-field convention.",
